    "The diocese name 'The Diocese of Kentucky' is inferred from Josh's own filename/roster identification, not printed anywhere in the source document's body -- same situation as this corpus's earlier Atlanta file.",
    "The source keys every entry to a liturgical Sunday name (e.g. 'First Sunday after the Epiphany'), Advent 2025 through Christ the King 2026, with NO calendar dates printed anywhere in the document. Calendar dates for the 2026 portion were determined by cross-referencing the Diocese of Lexington's own companion cycle-of-prayer document (also ingested in this same batch, file id 11SpVIKTeODLXHI5h6O4WB0fd9epvMZ6-), which covers the identical Advent 2025-Christ the King 2026 span using the same TEC 'Year A / Daily Office Year Two' lectionary and explicitly prints a calendar date for every one of the same Sunday names in the same order. Since both dioceses observe the same fixed Western liturgical calendar for the same civil year, this is a reliable date determination and not an independent computation -- it is disclosed here per this corpus's standing rule to flag any judgment call rather than presenting it as source-printed.",
    "Only the portion landing in calendar year 2026 (Second Sunday after Christmas through Christ the King, 47 Sundays, January 4 - November 22, 2026) is included; the four Advent 2025 Sundays and First Sunday after Christmas (2025) are out of this file's calendar-year scope.",
    "The source also prints four fixed-date commemorations interleaved between the weekly Sunday entries, unconnected to any specific Sunday's own subject: 'Thomas Merton is commemorated December 10' (2025, out of scope), 'Anne Braden, Civil Rights Activist is commemorated March 6' (2026, falls between the Second and Third Sundays in Lent), 'Sister Emily Cooper, Deaconess is commemorated on April 16' (2026, falls between the Second and Third Sundays of Easter), and 'A.H. McNeil, priest and educator, commemorated October 10' (2026, falls between the Eighteenth and Nineteenth Sundays after Pentecost). None is attached to a specific Sunday entry above, to avoid mis-attributing a fixed commemoration date to the wrong week; all are disclosed here in full instead.",
    "'Resurrection, Louisville' (May 31) and 'The School of Theology at Sewanee -- The University of the South' (April 26) are transcribed exactly as printed, including the source's own em-dash-style phrasing.",
    "Kentucky labels the Sunday before Lent 'Sixth Sunday after the Epiphany' where the BCP calendar and the Diocese of Lexington's own document instead call the same date 'Last Sunday after the Epiphany' -- both are the source's own choice of label for the identical date and are preserved as each diocese printed them."
